{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbv3vmGok1THVGvrRGPZ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7549, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7549}, "output_tokens": 9, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "763f9b77-ad34-4dd3-9cec-70626103b32d", "uuid": "9a90094f-ea87-4565-90d2-452083fa1f77", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T17:31:19.328Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbv3vPSMF84vQF5aw37M"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbv3vmGok1THVGvrRGPZ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The main difference is that **lists are mutable and tuples are immutable**. Most of the other differences come from that.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` or just `1, 2, 3` |\n| Mutable? | Yes. You can append, remove or reassign items | No. It's fixed once created |\n| Methods | Many (`append`, `extend`, `insert`, `pop`, `remove`, `sort`, `reverse`, \u2026) | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable? | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Memory/speed | A bit larger, with spare room for growth | Smaller and slightly faster to create |\n| Typical use | A collection of similar items that may change | A fixed record of related values |\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums.append(4)        # fine\nnums[0] = 99          # fine\n\npoint = (3, 4)\npoint[0] = 5          # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n\nlocations = {(40.7, -74.0): \"NYC\"}   # tuple as dict key: works\nlocations = {[40.7, -74.0]: \"NYC\"}   # TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n```\n\n**Things to watch out for:**\n- **A one-item tuple needs a trailing comma:** `(5,)` is a tuple, but `(5)` is just the integer `5`. The comma makes it a tuple, not the parentheses.\n- **Only the tuple itself is immutable, not what it contains:** `t = ([1], 2)` lets you run `t[0].append(3)`. Because it holds a list, `t` is also unhashable.\n- **Tuples are good for unpacking and returning several values:** for example `x, y = get_point()` or `return min_val, max_val`.\n- **Named tuples help with record-like data:** `collections.namedtuple` or `typing.NamedTuple` let you access fields by name, like `p.x`, and keep the tuple's immutability.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** use a list for a changing sequence of the same kind of thing, such as a list of users. Use a tuple for a fixed group of values where each position has its own meaning, such as `(latitude, longitude)` or `(name, age, email)`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7549, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7549}, "output_tokens": 9, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "763f9b77-ad34-4dd3-9cec-70626103b32d", "uuid": "4cbc1edf-482e-4918-b310-500ddb5dbd13", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T17:31:26.121Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbv3vPSMF84vQF5aw37M"}
